{
  "id": "gerpurp",
  "label": "Gerund and gerundive — expressing purpose",
  "short": "Gerundive purpose",
  "taughtAt": 39,
  "layout": {
    "rowLabels": [
      "ad + accusative",
      "genitive + causā/grātiā"
    ],
    "colLabels": [
      "form"
    ]
  },
  "notes": "Latin has four ways to say purpose and the infinitive is not one of them: ut/nē + subjunctive (Ch.28), ad + gerundive accusative, gerundive genitive + causā/grātiā, and the supine after verbs of motion (Ch.38). When the gerund would take a direct object, Latin prefers the gerundive agreeing with that object — 'gerundive attraction'.",
